        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7174fa34-93c8-5ada-95d1-705fa4a3151c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Croydon Airport, Gatwick Airport, Northolt Aerodrome, London Heathrow Airport, Brussels-Evere Airport, South London, south-east England, Central London, Crawley\n- Systems: ICAO, IATA\n- Topics: London airports, air transport, mail flights\n\nFacts:\n- Croydon Airport, with ICAO code EGCR, was an airport in South London, England.\n- Croydon Airport was the main airport for London before Northolt Aerodrome, London Heathrow Airport, and Gatwick Airport replaced it.\n- In the 1921 SNETA Farman Goliath ditching, the aircraft was operating a mail flight from Croydon Airport in the United Kingdom to Brussels-Evere Airport in Belgium.\n- Gatwick Airport, also known as London Gatwick, has IATA code LGW and ICAO code EGKK.\n- Gatwick Airport is a major international airport in south-east England, 29.5 miles south of Central London and 2.7 nautical miles north of Crawley.\n- Gatwick Airport is the eighth-busiest airport in Europe.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
